      "title": "M2.1 · Score ⇄ Sequence",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "M2.1 (with the 2026-11-05 feedback): Score to Sequence and back.\n\nA two-part score (flute, cello) with a tempo change (90, then 120 BPM in bar 3), dynamics, staccato/tenuto/accent, \"pizz.\"/\"arco\", a tie across the barline and a quarter-flat B in the flute.\n\nPress Run. Look at:\n1. Original score (top middle).\n2. Sequence Viewer: the same notes at their times in ms, one staff per part, timed by the score's own tempo marks (Score to Sequence has no bpm any more). The run after the tempo change is visibly faster. As in the score: dynamics in bold italic under the staff, pizz./arco in italics above it, staccato/tenuto/accent symbols on the notes, and the B drawn with a quarter-flat sign.\n3. Zoom (Sequence Viewer widget): change it and the viewer redraws at once, without pressing Run. 1 = 120 px per second.\n4. Back to a score: since M2.2 the Sequence carries the score's tempo map, so Sequence to Score with bpm 0 writes 90 BPM and then 120 BPM in bar 3, and quantizes on a sixteenth grid; the notes come back as written. (A Sequence without a map, e.g. from Orchidea, gets one estimated tempo.)\n\nTry: Sequence to Score bpm (0 = the Sequence's tempo map, else an estimate; another value forces one tempo) and quantization (\"none\" keeps exact times). Swap the path for any MusicXML/MIDI file: a file with no tempo mark is read at 120 BPM."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
